      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Civil liberties groups often rely on the logic that the state should not have the authority to dictate what individuals put into their own bodies, viewing mandatory vaccines as a dangerous precedent for government overreach. They argue that if the government can force you to vaccinate, it sets a slippery slope where future mandates could extend to other areas of personal life, fundamentally eroding the social contract. While they may not always have a perfect scientific counter-argument, their core stance is that individual bodily autonomy is a sacred right that cannot be traded off for the statistical safety of the herd. Ultimately, they believe that a free society must be willing to accept the risks of disease outbreaks rather than surrendering personal freedom to the state.",
      "source": "target",
